MEDICAL DISCLAIMER (READ FIRST)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

This software is NOT a medical device.

It is provided strictly as a personal-research and data-management tool. It
does not diagnose, treat, cure, mitigate, or prevent any disease or medical
condition. None of its computed metrics, scores, classifications, plots, or
LLM-generated text constitute medical advice.

Do not use this software to make medical decisions. Always consult a qualified
healthcare professional. The authors disclaim all liability for any direct or
indirect harm resulting from use of, or reliance on, this software or its
outputs. THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND,
EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING WITHOUT LIMITATION THE WARRANTIES OF
MERCHANTABILITY AND FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.

In particular: arrhythmia detection, POTS criterion, PEM correlation,
SpO2 burden, ANS status, HRV change-points, VLM-based image analysis (e.g. of
a skin lesion or fundus photo), and any other clinical-sounding output is
heuristic, derived from consumer-grade sensors, and unsuitable for diagnosis.
Consumer wearables are not regulated medical devices; their measurements have
substantial error margins.

This software CAN produce incorrect results in both directions. A false
positive can suggest a problem where none exists. A false negative can fail
to flag a real problem. Neither outcome is bounded or quantified for any
given output of this software; treat every result, positive or negative, as
provisional and unverified until reviewed by a qualified healthcare
professional.

A qualified healthcare professional, not this software or any AI/LLM
component within it, holds sole diagnostic and decision-making authority.
Nothing produced by this software substitutes for that professional's
judgment, and no output should ever be the basis for a medical decision on
its own. The user is solely responsible for the consequences of any action,
or inaction, taken on the basis of this software's output.

Findings surfaced by this software, especially by VLM-based image analysis
(for example, a skin lesion flagged as high-risk), can be distressing and can
require tolerating real uncertainty until a clinician resolves the question.
This is an inherent psychological cost of using this software, not a defect
to be engineered away. Users should consider their own emotional readiness
before running an analysis they suspect may surface an unsettling finding,
and should involve a trusted person or professional support if needed.

The guided anamnesis interview (`scripts/query/anamnese_interview.py`,
LLM-driven, multi-session) is not rock-solid. It has not yet been validated
under real-conditions dogfooding for whether its associative follow-up
questions actually surface leads a careful manual review would have found,
whether its memory-anchor prompting works as intended, or whether it stays
consistent in one language over a long session. Its output can be
incomplete, can miss something relevant, or can misrepresent what you said.
Treat everything it produces as a draft for you to review, not a finished
or reliable anamnesis.

REVIEW OF THE UNDERLYING LITERATURE IS STILL PENDING. Scientific citations
embedded throughout this codebase (see the "Evidence tier" annotations in
docs/, and the citations listed per compute/analysis script) were added
ad hoc as each script was written. There has been no dedicated pass to
verify that every citation is current, correctly represents the cited
study's findings, or that no more relevant or more recent literature was
missed. This review had been the main thing blocking release for the past
weeks. The decision was to release now with this gap open rather than
delay further, so as many people as possible can benefit from Kyoro as
soon as possible — even with citations that may still be imprecise or
wrong. It is on the project's TODO list and is the next thing being
tackled. Treat every such citation as unverified until that review is
done.

This software has NOT been evaluated under EU MDR (2017/745), FDA 21 CFR 820,
or any other medical-device regulatory framework.


────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DATA SOVEREIGNTY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

This software is designed for fully local operation. By default:

  - The databases live on your local filesystem (default: ~/Kyoro-HealthHub/data/health.db
    for wearable/sensor time series and raw genetics, ~/Kyoro-HealthHub/data/medicine.db
    for clinical values a doctor measures or orders)
  - Raw data exports (CSV, XML, JSON, PDF) live on your local filesystem
  - Configuration (API tokens, personal data) lives in ~/.config/kyoro/ — outside the repository
  - No telemetry, analytics, or background uploads are performed by this software

External network traffic ONLY happens when:

  - You configure an importer that pulls from a manufacturer API (currently,
    for example: Garmin Connect, Polar AccessLink, Oura, Home Assistant —
    see `docs/DEVICES.md` for the full and current list). These calls are
    authenticated against YOUR account and use YOUR personal access tokens.
    No third party sees the data.
  - You explicitly invoke an LLM-assisted script (e.g. `scripts/medical/tables_llm.py`,
    `scripts/query/health_query.py`) configured to use a remote model. The
    default configuration uses a LOCAL inference provider (OpenVINO by
    default; MLX, Ollama, llama.cpp, LM Studio, OVMS, and exo are also
    supported, see `scripts/utils/llm_provider.py`). Switching to a remote
    provider (OpenRouter, Anthropic, Mistral, Azure, and others — see
    `scripts/utils/llm_provider.py` for the full list) is opt-in and
    transmits the data you select to that provider.

You are responsible for:
  - Securing your local database file (it contains highly sensitive personal data)
  - Managing API tokens and access credentials
  - Choosing whether to enable remote LLM providers, and accepting their privacy terms
  - Backups (this software performs none)

This software contains no automatic or background mechanism to share data
outside your machine. Explicit, user-initiated exports — for example, generating
a summary for a cardiologist or rheumatologist via `export_health.py` — are an
intentional feature. All such exports are fully under your control: you decide
what to export, in what format, and to whom you hand it.


